From: Andrei Gosman <andrei.gosman@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 08 Sep 2026 10:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 005] fix: provide osmo_cpu_sched_vty_init() when the file is Linux only

This port wraps src/vty/cpu_sched_vty.c in #ifdef __linux__, because the
whole cpu-sched VTY node rests on sched_setaffinity and the Linux scheduling
policies. That also removes osmo_cpu_sched_vty_init(), which is public and
which every Osmocom daemon calls during startup, so linking osmo-stp failed
with an undefined symbol.

Add an #else branch defining the initialiser as a no-op. It installs
nothing, so the cpu-sched node does not exist on this platform and a
configuration file carrying a cpu-sched block is rejected. That is the
honest outcome: the underlying scheduling controls are not there to
configure.
---
diff --git a/src/vty/cpu_sched_vty.c b/src/vty/cpu_sched_vty.c
index a712860b..cf2c18a8 100644
--- a/src/vty/cpu_sched_vty.c
+++ b/src/vty/cpu_sched_vty.c
@@ -1,3 +1,4 @@
+#ifdef __linux__
 /*! \file cpu_sched_vty.c
  * Implementation to CPU / Threading / Scheduler properties from VTY configuration.
  */
@@ -689,3 +690,17 @@ int osmo_cpu_sched_vty_apply_localthread(void)
 }
 
 /*! @} */
+#else /* !__linux__ */
+
+/* The whole cpu-sched VTY node rests on sched_setaffinity and the Linux
+ * scheduling policies, which Darwin does not have. The public initialiser
+ * still has to exist, because every Osmocom daemon calls it at startup.
+ * Installing nothing leaves the cpu-sched node absent, so a configuration
+ * file that carries a cpu-sched block is rejected on this platform. */
+int osmo_cpu_sched_vty_init(void *tall_ctx)
+{
+	(void)tall_ctx;
+	return 0;
+}
+
+#endif /* __linux__ */
